{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And Moses said to Korah: You and all your assembly, be before HaShem</b>. Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] (on Numbers 16:16) wrote: this has already been stated (above, verses 5–7), but the meaning is that just as Moses said to Korah, 'Be before HaShem,' each man took his firepan. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that at first he said to them: 'Do this: take for yourselves firepans, Korah and all his assembly, etc., and the man whom HaShem will choose, he is the holy one' — and he did not say that Aaron would be with them. And they remained silent, or perhaps they were not satisfied and did not concede this point, for they reasoned: if Aaron is with us, the fire will descend upon all of us equally or it will not descend at all, and we will all be equal. And if Aaron is not with us and the fire does not descend, they will say that He did not choose us, and they will suppose that He had already chosen him, since the fire descended upon his offerings on the eighth day. And on that day we have a claim against him — that it was not fitting to choose Aaron to be the sole priest, but rather it was in the merit of Israel who made the Tabernacle, and the fire would have descended for any emissary of the congregation, and at that time the firstborn had not yet been replaced. Therefore Korah did not wish to put the matter to the test without Aaron. And Dathan and Abiram were there as well; when Moses spoke to them accordingly, they were included in what he said, 'Korah and all his assembly' (Numbers 16:6). And when he sent for them and they said, 'We will not go up,' Moses became very angry and wished to decree upon them that they would not die the death of all men. Then Moses turned back to Korah and said: 'Take each man his firepan, two hundred and fifty firepans, and you and Aaron, each man his firepan.' Now he excluded Dathan and Abiram from their number. And this is the meaning of 'two hundred and fifty firepans,' for what need is there to mention the count in this place? And he brought Aaron in to be with them. Then Korah listened to him, for he thought that the matter would be equal between them and Aaron."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Ibn Ezra by his epithet 'Rabbi Abraham'; the parenthetical reference to Ibn Ezra's commentary is editorial in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:13.866513581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}